{
  "Every control follows the same rules for Tab order, the active control of a container, focus handling, `&File` access keys and application-wide shortcuts.": "",
  "Every control is a Tab stop unless `tabStop = false`. `tabIndex` sets the order of the controls in a container.": "",
  "Containers (`FocusContainer`) track `activeControl` and restore focus with `restoreFocus()` when they become active again.": "",
  "One Tab stop with arrow keys inside, using a roving tabindex or `aria-activedescendant`. This arrives with the first composite control.": "",
  "When the focused part of a control is disabled, hidden, removed or disposed, focus moves to the next usable element.": "",
  "Shown only during keyboard use (`:focus-visible`), with the design system's double ring.": "",
  "`&Save` in a control's text assigns Alt+S. The letter is underlined while Alt is held.": "",
  "`addShortcut('Ctrl+S', …)` registers a shortcut for the whole application or for one control.": "",
  "Tab order": "Tab sırası",
  "Give controls a `tabIndex`, and the outermost container of the page or window visits them in ascending order at each level of the control tree. Equal indexes and plain elements (index 0) follow document order. Until a control sets a `tabIndex`, Tab is handled by the browser. A modal window keeps Tab inside itself, and only one radio button per group is a Tab stop, as in browsers.": "",
  "Active control": "Etkin control",
  "A `FocusContainer` knows which child control holds focus (`activeControl`, reactive) and returns focus to it with `restoreFocus()`. For example, when a window comes to the front or the user returns to a tab page.": "",
  "Access keys": "Erişim tuşları",
  "Put `&` before a letter: `&Save`, `Save &as…`. `&&` produces a literal ampersand. Only the first marker counts.": "",
  "Alt plus the key. On Apple platforms, use Control + Option plus the key, because Option alone types characters there. Browsers use the same keys for HTML `accesskey`.": "",
  "The key activates the control. A button receives focus and is clicked, as with HTML `accesskey`. Any other control receives focus (`onAccessKey()`).": "",
  "Each press moves focus to the next match without activating it.": "",
  "The modal window if one is open, otherwise the window that has focus, otherwise the page outside any window. Disabled and hidden controls are skipped.": "",
  "AltGr (Ctrl+Alt on Windows) is ignored, because it types characters such as `@` on a Turkish keyboard.": "",
  "Shown while Alt is held (Control + Option on Apple platforms). Tapping Alt keeps the underlines visible until Alt is tapped again, another key is pressed or the pointer is used. While the page has access keys, that tap is not passed to the browser, which would otherwise move keyboard focus to its menu bar. Set `data-mx-access-keys=\"always\"` on `<html>` to show them all the time.": "",
  "The accessible name is the plain text, and the control receives `aria-keyshortcuts=\"Alt+S\"` (`\"Control+Alt+S\"` on Apple platforms).": "",
  "Any element with `aria-keyshortcuts=\"Alt+N\"` takes part. A button-like element is clicked, an input receives focus, and a `<label>` moves focus to its field.": "",
  "Browsers reserve some Alt combinations for their own menus, and these vary by browser and language. Test the keys you choose.": "",
  "Shortcuts": "Kısayollar",
  "Key combinations": "",
  "The Ctrl key.": "Ctrl tuşu.",
  "The Alt key (Option on a Mac).": "Alt tuşu (Mac'te Option).",
  "The Shift key.": "Shift tuşu.",
  "The Windows key, or Cmd on a Mac.": "",
  "Cmd on Apple platforms and Ctrl elsewhere, so `Mod+S` saves on every platform.": "",
  "A character (`S`, `1`, `/`) or a `KeyboardEvent.key` name: `Enter`, `Escape` (`Esc`), `Delete` (`Del`), `F2`, `ArrowUp` (`Up`), `Space`, `Plus`… Case does not matter. `Ctrl++` means Ctrl and plus.": "",
  "Shortcut precedence": "",
  "Shortcuts scoped to the controls that hold focus run first, starting with the innermost. Application-wide shortcuts run last. A handler that returns `false` passes the key on.": "",
  "While a modal window is open, only shortcuts scoped inside it run. Application-wide shortcuts are suppressed.": "",
  "Application-wide shortcuts without Ctrl, Alt or Meta (Delete, Escape, a letter) do not run while focus is in a window, because those keys belong to the window. Combinations with a modifier and function keys still run.": "",
  "A combination without Ctrl, Alt or Meta does not fire in text fields, so that Delete deletes text, unless you set `inEditable: true`. Function keys and combinations with a modifier work everywhere.": "",
  "A shortcut scoped to a disabled control does not run.": "Devre dışı bir control'e bağlı kısayol çalışmaz.",
  "The key receives `preventDefault()` and goes no further. Shortcuts run before access keys, so an explicit combination takes priority over an implicit key.": "",
  "Focus never lands on &lt;body&gt;": "",
  "When the focused part of a control is disabled, hidden, removed (a list row, a tree branch) or disposed, focus moves to the next usable element from that position in the same window, or to the previous one. Clicking an empty area still clears focus as usual, and route changes are left to the router.": "",
  "Return value / type": "",
  "Registers a shortcut and returns a function that removes it. `options.scope` limits it to a component, and `options.inEditable` also enables it in text fields.": "",
  "Registers a shortcut scoped to the control and removes it when the control is disposed.": "",
  "Parses `&Text` for your own controls. Render the result with the `mx-access-key` class and call `setAccessKey(key)`.": "",
  "prop / property": "prop / property",
  "The order inside the container. The default is 0.": "",
  "See the Control page.": "Control sayfasına bakın.",
  "For `tabStop`, `tabIndex`, `focus()`, `onAccessKey()` and `FocusContainer`, see the `Control` page.": ""
}
